Torsten Zirzlaff <zirzlaff@nienor.s.bawue.de> writes:

> Hi.
>
> Are those 64bit PCI slots in a PWS500au dead or it is possible to
> populate them with regular 32bit PCI cards? I know that you should not
> use 'em for your SCSI-controller or graphics card. I think about using
> a firewire or usb controller, but fear that those cards will not work
> in those slots and the regular 32bit ones are all taken.

Any PCI card is required to work in any slot it can physically be
inserted into.  This means that a 32-bit card will work in a 64-bit
slot, and a 64-bit card in a 32-bit slot (with the obvious
limitation).  This is all in the absence of bugs, of course.

As we know, the Miata/PWS has a DMA bug affecting its 64-bit slots,
making them ill-suited for things doing large DMA transfers to the
card, disk controllers coming to mind here.  Things not using DMA are
all safe, as are some cards that only use DMA in a safe way.  Using
the 64-bit slots for graphics cards is usually OK.  They don't use DMA
most of the time, and even if they do, nothing worse than a few
incorrect pixels would happen.

In short, don't use the 64-bit slots for anything involving data
storage in any way.  Anything else is probably OK.

